<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Drive SH 121 and I-35E at the hours you would actually commute before choosing a side of town. The difference between a reverse commute and a peak-direction drive can change which community makes sense.</p></div>

<h2>What to Look for in a 2-Bedroom Layout</h2>
<p>Two-bedroom floor plans are not all alike, and the right one depends on who is living there.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Split bedrooms</strong> put the bedrooms on opposite sides of the living area. They are great for roommates or for a home office you want separated from where someone sleeps.</li>
<li><strong>Adjacent bedrooms</strong> keep rooms close together, which many parents with young kids prefer.</li>
<li><strong>Two full bathrooms</strong> make mornings easier for roommates and couples on different schedules.</li>
<li><strong>Storage and laundry.</strong> A coat closet, linen closet and in-unit washer and dryer connections matter more than you think with two bedrooms' worth of stuff.</li>
<li><strong>Outdoor space.</strong> A patio or balcony adds usable room in good weather, especially in North Texas spring and fall.</li>
</ul>
<p>Bring a tape measure to your tour and check the second bedroom against the furniture you already own. Marketing square footage rarely tells you whether a queen bed and a desk will both fit.</p>

<h2>Making the Most of the Second Bedroom</h2>
<p>A second bedroom can be a home office, guest room, nursery or roommate space. If it is doing double duty, [[amz:under_bed_storage|under-bed storage]] keeps seasonal clothes and supplies out of sight, and a [[amz:storage_ottoman|storage ottoman]] adds a seat without crowding the room.</p>
<p>Moving into a bigger place usually means moving more stuff. Plan ahead with good boxes and a way to shift heavy furniture without scratching the floors.</p>
